 ## Run it
 
-The game must be served over HTTP, because every source file is an ES module and
-browsers refuse to import modules from `file://` (that is a security rule, not a
-quirk of this project). Double-clicking `index.html` will show a blank page and a
-console error about module scripts.
+Every source file is an ES module, and browsers refuse to import modules from
+`file://` (a security rule, not a quirk of this project). Double-clicking
+`index.html` gives a blank page and a console error. So serve it:
 
-From the project root:
-
 ```bash
 python3 -m http.server 8000
 ```
 
-Then open <http://localhost:8000>. Any other static server works the same way
-(`npx serve .`, `php -S localhost:8000`, Live Server in VS Code).
+Then open <http://localhost:8000>. Any static server works (`npx serve .`,
+`php -S localhost:8000`, Live Server in VS Code).
 
-There is nothing to install and nothing to build. Three.js is vendored in
+Nothing to install, nothing to build. Three.js is vendored in
 `js/vendor/three.module.js`, every sound is synthesised at runtime by
-`js/core/audio.js`, and the game never makes a network request unless you paste a
-Google Analytics id into `js/core/config.js`. The browser gates in the Tests section
-expect the game on port **8765** instead: `python3 -m http.server 8765`.
+`js/core/audio.js`, and the game makes no network request unless you paste a Google
+Analytics id into `js/core/config.js`. The browser gates in the Tests section expect
+the game on port **8765** instead: `python3 -m http.server 8765`.
 
